llm_answer:
It looks like you have a solid start to your interactive check-in page with popups. To address your implementation, let’s enhance your pop-up functionality so that the scripture appears when the user clicks on the emotion they selected. 

### Steps to Fix the Popup Implementation

1. **HTML Structure**: Make sure each emotion div has its own unique ID for the popup. The example you have only shows the happy emotion, so you'll likely want to extend this for other emotions as well. 

2. **CSS Styles**: Ensure you have the necessary styles to hide and show the popups.

3. **JavaScript Functionality**: The `myFunction` should be improved to accommodate different emotions and ensure that only one popup is shown at a time.

### Updated Code Example

Here’s how you can structure your code:

#### HTML

```html
<div class="emotion-wrapper">
  <div class="emotion thumb" onclick="showPopup('happyPopup')">
    <p>Happy</p>
  </div>
  <div class="emotion thumb" onclick="showPopup('sadPopup')">
    <p>Sad</p>
  </div>
  <div class="emotion thumb" onclick="showPopup('excitedPopup')">
    <p>Excited</p>
  </div>

  <div class="popup" id="happyPopup">
    <span class="popup-content">I praise you, for I am fearfully and wonderfully made. Wonderful are your works; my soul knows it very well. Psalm 139:14</span>
    <button onclick="closePopup('happyPopup')">Close</button>
  </div>

  <div class="popup" id="sadPopup">
    <span class="popup-content">The Lord is near to the brokenhearted and saves the crushed in spirit. Psalm 34:18</span>
    <button onclick="closePopup('sadPopup')">Close</button>
  </div>

  <div class="popup" id="excitedPopup">
    <span class="popup-content">This is the day that the Lord has made; let us rejoice and be glad in it. Psalm 118:24</span>
    <button onclick="closePopup('excitedPopup')">Close</button>
  </div>
</div>
```

#### CSS

```css
.popup {
  display: none; /* Initially hidden */
  position: absolute; /* You may want to change this based on your layout */
  background-color: white;
  border: 1px solid black;
  padding: 10px;
  z-index: 1000;
}

.popup.show {
  display: block; /* Show the popup when the show class is added */
}
```

#### JavaScript

```javascript
function showPopup(popupId) {
  // First, close any currently open popups
  var popups = document.querySelectorAll('.popup');
  popups.forEach(function(popup) {
    popup.classList.remove('show');
  });

  // Then, show the selected popup
  var popup = document.getElementById(popupId);
  popup.classList.add('show');
}

function closePopup(popupId) {
  var popup = document.getElementById(popupId);
  popup.classList.remove('show');
}
```

### Explanation

1. **HTML**: Each emotion now triggers the `showPopup` function with the corresponding popup ID as an argument. Each popup div has a unique ID.

2. **CSS**: The `.popup` class controls the visibility of the popups using `display: none;` and `display: block;` when the popup is active.

3. **JavaScript**:
   - `showPopup` function: First, it hides all currently open popups by removing the `show` class. Then, it adds the `show` class to the desired popup.
   - `closePopup` function: This function can be called when the user clicks the close button to hide the popup.

### Conclusion

By implementing the steps above, you should now have a fully functioning interactive check-in page with popups showing scripts that correspond to the selected emotions.  Feel free to expand upon this by adding styles and structuring the HTML to meet your design preferences!

